{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015qQ2Yoiba3BRUGZRyKsEcQ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'Core framing (\"item dominance + benchmark gradient\") collapses.\n2\t  Reframe around: \"Model×item interaction is the hidden dominant variance source in\n3\t  LLM evaluation — items that are easy for one model are hard for another, and this\n4\t  interaction, not item difficulty alone, drives irreproducibility.\" Separability\n5\t  failure becomes a positive finding: isolated-factor studies are insufficient. D-study\n6\t  pivots to \"current evaluation practices are even more inadequate than prior work\n7\t  suggests.\" Drop the gradient narrative entirely; replace with \"variance structure\n8\t  is benchmark-specific and unpredictable from task taxonomy alone.\" The paper becomes\n9\t  more iconoclastic. ACL ARR October becomes realistic primary target.\n10\t\n11\t  '\n12\timagined_experiments:\n13\t- name: exp-001 Full-Scale Precision Study (MMLU)\n14\t  plan_id: exp-001\n15\t  description: '4 models × 3 precisions × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 3 temperatures × 4\n16\t    orderings × 200 MMLU items. Gemma-2-9B-IT fails during FP16 runs (known from registry:\n17\t    exp001_gemma FAILED). Qwen3-8B produces sporadic decoding anomalies under FP32\n18\t    at T=0.7, requiring partial reruns. Final dataset covers 3 models (Llama, Mistral,\n19\t    Qwen) with Gemma fully excluded, yielding 3 × 1296 = 3888 conditions × 200 items\n20\t    = 777,600 records.\n21\t\n22\t    '\n23\t  expected_results:\n24\t    total_records: 777600\n25\t    models_completed: 3\n26\t    models_excluded: 1\n27\t    conditions_per_model: 1296\n28\t    items_per_condition: 200\n29\t    overall_accuracy_llama: 0.681\n30\t    overall_accuracy_mistral: 0.623\n31\t    overall_accuracy_qwen: 0.704\n32\t    accuracy_bfloat16_pooled: 0.68\n33\t    accuracy_float16_pooled: 0.678\n34\t    accuracy_float32_pooled: 0.611\n35\t    float32_degradation_pp: 6.8\n36\t    null_predictions_pct: 0.03\n37\t    item_id_pct_llama_single: 0.561\n38\t    item_id_pct_mistral_single: 0.534\n39\t    item_id_pct_qwen_single: 0.589\n40\t    residual_pct_llama_single: 0.182\n41\t    residual_pct_mistral_single: 0.197\n42\t    residual_pct_qwen_single: 0.168\n43\t    g_coeff_binary_llama_200item: 0.362\n44\t    g_coeff_binary_mistral_200item: 0.338\n45\t    g_coeff_binary_qwen_200item: 0.371\n46\t    g_coeff_text_match_llama_200item: 0.968\n47\t    precision_x_temperature_text_match_llama_pct: 1.5\n48\t    precision_x_temperature_text_match_mistral_pct: 1.8\n49\t  result_rationale: 'Single-model analyses replicate MVP findings: item_id dominates\n50\t    at 53-59% per model, consistent with Bernoulli variance spread across 200 items\n51\t    of heterogeneous difficulty. FP32 degradation (~6.8pp) replicates the V0 enforce_eager-controlled\n52\t    pattern from bf16_eager_disentangle. These per-model results look encouraging\n53\t    — item dominance appears stable. But this is misleading: the residual component\n54\t    (17-20%) is inflated because model×item interaction is confounded with residual\n55\t    when model is not a facet. The true picture emerges only in exp-004 reanalysis.\n56\t    Gemma exclusion reduces the model universe from 4 to 3, weakening generalizability\n57\t    claims and inviting reviewer criticism about restricted model coverage. Qwen FP32\n58\t    anomalies at T=0.7 suggest architecture-specific precision sensitivity, foreshadowing\n59\t    the idiosyncratic variance patterns that dominate the worst-tier narrative.\n60\t\n61\t    '\n62\t  tables:\n63\t  - name: 'Table 1: Single-Model Variance Decomposition (MMLU, 200 items, binary scoring)'\n64\t    description: 'Rows: variance sources (item_id, prompt, seed, temperature, ordering,\n65\t      precision, all 2-way interactions, residual). Columns: Llama, Mistral, Qwen,\n66\t      Pooled. Shows item_id dominant at 53-59% across all 3 models. Residual inflated\n67\t      at 17-20%. Precision×temperature interaction 0.3-0.5% under binary, 1.5-1.8%\n68\t      under text_match.\n69\t\n70\t      '\n71\t  - name: 'Table 2: Accuracy Summary by Precision × Temperature × Model'\n72\t    description: '3×3×3 table. FP32 degradation visible across all models (5.5-8.5pp).\n73\t      Temperature effect modest (T=0.0 vs T=0.7: 2-3pp). Cross-model accuracy spread:\n74\t      8pp.\n75\t\n76\t      '\n77\t  figures_description:\n78\t  - Accuracy heatmap (3 models × 3 precisions × 3 temperatures). FP32 row visibly\n79\t    darker. Model ranking preserved across conditions but gap varies.\n80\t  - 'Item-level accuracy distributions per model: overlapping but offset histograms\n81\t    showing each model has distinct difficulty profile for the same 200 items.'\n82\t  analysis: 'Data collection succeeds for 3/4 models. Per-model G-studies replicate\n83\t    MVP item dominance finding. However, item-level correlation between models is\n84\t    only r=0.71-0.78 (not r=0.90+), foreshadowing large model×item interaction in\n85\t    the facet reanalysis. The single-model framing looks solid at this stage but will\n86\t    collapse in exp-004.\n87\t\n88\t    '\n89\t- name: exp-001b Item Resampling Stability\n90\t  plan_id: exp-001b\n91\t  description: '3 independent stratified 200-item MMLU samples (seeds 0, 7, 99) ×\n92\t    Llama × 3 precisions × 3 prompts × 3 seeds × 2 orderings = 54 conditions per sample.\n93\t    Tests whether item_id dominance percentage is stable across item draws.\n94\t\n95\t    '\n96\t  expected_results:\n97\t    total_records_per_sample: 10800\n98\t    total_records: 32400\n99\t    item_id_pct_sample_seed0: 0.541\n100\t    item_id_pct_sample_seed7: 0.612\n101\t    item_id_pct_sample_seed99: 0.487\n102\t    item_id_range_pp: 12.5\n103\t    residual_pct_range:\n104\t    - 0.155\n105\t    - 0.221\n106\t    g_coeff_binary_seed0: 0.341\n107\t    g_coeff_binary_seed7: 0.389\n108\t    g_coeff_binary_seed99: 0.312\n109\t    g_coeff_range_pp: 7.7\n110\t    item_difficulty_mean_seed0: 0.683\n111\t    item_difficulty_mean_seed7: 0.671\n112\t    item_difficulty_mean_seed99: 0.698\n113\t    item_difficulty_sd_seed0: 0.284\n114\t    item_difficulty_sd_seed7: 0.301\n115\t    item_difficulty_sd_seed99: 0.262\n116\t  result_rationale: 'The 12.5pp range in item_id percentage across three samples (48.7-61.2%)\n117\t    is uncomfortably wide. This is driven by differential item difficulty variance:\n118\t    sample seed99 has lower difficulty SD (0.262 vs 0.301), mechanically reducing\n119\t    Bernoulli p(1-p) spread and thus item_id''s share. This directly validates the\n120\t    MD1 concern — item_id percentage is partly a mechanical artifact of the item difficulty\n121\t    distribution in the sample, not a stable psychometric property. The instability\n122\t    makes it impossible to report a single \"item_id = X%\" headline number with confidence.\n123\t    In the worst tier, this is compounded by the fact that model×item interaction\n124\t    (not measurable in single-model exp-001b) will further erode whatever item_id\n125\t    estimate survives. Bootstrap CIs on item_id will be wide (±6-8pp per sample),\n126\t    and the cross-sample range exceeds the within-sample CI, indicating genuine instability\n127\t    rather than sampling noise alone.\n128\t\n129\t    '\n130\t  tables:\n131\t  - name: 'Table 3: Variance Decomposition Stability Across Item Samples'\n132\t    description: 'Rows: variance sources. Columns: Sample A (seed0), Sample B (seed7),\n133\t      Sample C (seed99), Range. Item_id ranges 48.7-61.2%. Residual ranges 15.5-22.1%.\n134\t      Prompt×item ranges 4.8-7.2%. Instability is systematic, not random.\n135\t\n136\t      '\n137\t  figures_description:\n138\t  - Three-panel stacked bar chart showing variance decomposition for each item sample.\n139\t    Visible structural differences despite identical design and model.\n140\t  - 'Scatter plot: item difficulty SD vs item_id percentage across samples, showing\n141\t    positive correlation (mechanical Bernoulli link).'\n142\t  analysis: 'Item dominance magnitude is sample-dependent, with a 12.5pp range across\n143\t    three draws. This is a serious threat to the headline claim. The Bernoulli variance\n144\t    mechanism (MD1) explains ~60% of the cross-sample variation. Combined with the\n145\t    model×item interaction finding from exp-004, the \"item dominance\" narrative becomes\n146\t    untenable as a clean headline. The paper must reframe: item_id is large but its\n147\t    magnitude is unstable and partly mechanical; the more interesting finding is what\n148\t    happens when model is added as a facet.\n149\t\n150\t    '\n151\t- name: exp-002 Multi-Benchmark Replication\n152\t  plan_id: exp-002\n153\t  description: '3 models (Gemma excluded) × 2 precisions (BF16/FP16) × 6 prompts ×\n154\t    6 seeds × 2 temperatures × 4 orderings × 200 items each on GSM8K, ARC-Challenge,\n155\t    HellaSwag. Reduced crossing (2×2 vs 3×3). Tests cross-benchmark gradient hypothesis.\n156\t\n157\t    '\n158\t  expected_results:\n159\t    total_records_gsm8k: 172800\n160\t    total_records_arc: 172800\n161\t    total_records_hellaswag: 172800\n162\t    models_completed_gsm8k: 3\n163\t    models_completed_arc: 2\n164\t    models_completed_hellaswag: 3\n165\t    benchmarks_with_complete_data: 3\n166\t    item_id_pct_gsm8k_pooled: 0.143\n167\t    seed_pct_gsm8k: 0.089\n168\t    temperature_pct_gsm8k: 0.112\n169\t    prompt_pct_gsm8k: 0.067\n170\t    seed_x_item_pct_gsm8k: 0.138\n171\t    temperature_x_item_pct_gsm8k: 0.124\n172\t    residual_pct_gsm8k: 0.247\n173\t    precision_x_temperature_gsm8k_binary_pct: 3.2\n174\t    g_coeff_binary_gsm8k_200item: 0.198\n175\t    item_id_pct_arc_pooled: 0.472\n176\t    seed_pct_arc: 0.018\n177\t    temperature_pct_arc: 0.031\n178\t    prompt_pct_arc: 0.093\n179\t    residual_pct_arc: 0.196\n180\t    precision_x_temperature_arc_binary_pct: 0.4\n181\t    g_coeff_binary_arc_200item: 0.311\n182\t    item_id_pct_hellaswag_pooled: 0.387\n183\t    seed_pct_hellaswag: 0.042\n184\t    temperature_pct_hellaswag: 0.058\n185\t    prompt_pct_hellaswag: 0.121\n186\t    precision_x_prompt_hellaswag_binary_pct: 3.8\n187\t    residual_pct_hellaswag: 0.203\n188\t    g_coeff_binary_hellaswag_200item: 0.274\n189\t    cross_benchmark_gradient_pp: 5.1\n190\t    item_id_ordering_by_benchmark: ARC(47.2%) > HellaSwag(38.7%) > MMLU(~55%) > GSM8K(14.3%)\n191\t    gradient_correlation_with_task_type: -0.12\n192\t    direct_infra_x_method_binary_gsm8k: 3.2\n193\t    direct_infra_x_method_binary_hellaswag: 3.8\n194\t    direct_infra_x_method_binary_arc: 0.4\n195\t    direct_infra_x_method_text_match_gsm8k: 7.4\n196\t    direct_infra_x_method_text_match_hellaswag: 5.9\n197\t    benchmarks_separability_fails_binary: 2\n198\t  result_rationale: 'The cross-benchmark gradient hypothesis collapses. The item_id\n199\t    ordering is MMLU(55%) > ARC(47%) > HellaSwag(39%) > GSM8K(14%), which does not\n200\t    follow any coherent task taxonomy. ARC (science reasoning) has higher item_id\n201\t    than HellaSwag (commonsense), despite commonsense items being more \"knowledge-like.\"\n202\t    GSM8K''s low item_id (14.3%) is expected — math reasoning items have high seed×item\n203\t    and temperature×item interactions because chain-of-thought generation introduces\n204\t    stochastic branching at each reasoning step. But HellaSwag''s intermediate position\n205\t    and ARC''s high position are not predicted by any task-type theory. The correlation\n206\t    between task-type ranking and item_id ranking is near zero (r=-0.12), destroying\n207\t    the \"knowledge→reasoning gradient\" narrative.\n208\t\n209\t    Separability fails on GSM8K (precision×temperature = 3.2% under binary) and HellaSwag\n210\t    (precision×prompt = 3.8%). The GSM8K failure is interpretable: at T=0.7, floating-point\n211\t    non-associativity in the softmax creates different token selection paths across\n212\t    precisions, and for multi-step reasoning, these divergences compound. The HellaSwag\n213\t    failure is less interpretable — precision×prompt interaction suggests that certain\n214\t    prompt framings expose precision-dependent attention patterns in the commonsense\n215\t    completion task. Neither failure was predicted by the MVP data (which showed separability\n216\t    under binary scoring on MMLU). This means the Claim 2 separability result is benchmark-conditional\n217\t    in a way that undermines confident generalization.\n218\t\n219\t    The residual components are large everywhere (19.6-24.7%), foreshadowing the model×item\n220\t    interaction that will emerge when model is treated as a facet in exp-004.\n221\t\n222\t    '\n223\t  tables:\n224\t  - name: 'Table 4: Variance Decomposition Across Benchmarks (Single-Model Pooled,\n225\t      Binary Scoring)'\n226\t    description: 'Rows: 10 variance sources. Columns: MMLU, GSM8K, ARC-Challenge,\n227\t      HellaSwag. No systematic gradient visible. Item_id ranges from 14.3% (GSM8K)\n228\t      to 55%+ (MMLU). Residual uniformly large (19-25%). Interaction terms vary idiosyncratically.\n229\t\n230\t      '\n231\t  - name: 'Table 5: Infrastructure × Methodology Interactions (Binary + Text Match)'\n232\t    description: 'All 2-way infra×method interactions per benchmark under both scoring\n233\t      metrics. Binary: GSM8K 3.2%, HellaSwag 3.8%, ARC 0.4%, MMLU 0.3%. Text match:\n234\t      GSM8K 7.4%, HellaSwag 5.9%. Separability fails on 2/4 benchmarks under binary.\n235\t\n236\t      '\n237\t  - name: 'Table 6: G-coefficients by Benchmark and Metric'\n238\t    description: 'Binary G: MMLU 0.36, ARC 0.31, HellaSwag 0.27, GSM8K 0.20. Text\n239\t      match G: MMLU 0.97, GSM8K 0.88, ARC 0.91, HellaSwag 0.93. Binary G is poor everywhere;\n240\t      text match is acceptable but variable.\n241\t\n242\t      '\n243\t  figures_description:\n244\t  - Stacked-bar variance decomposition across 4 benchmarks. The signature figure —\n245\t    visually demonstrates NO gradient. Each benchmark has a unique 'fingerprint' of\n246\t    variance sources.\n247\t  - Radar chart of top-5 variance sources per benchmark, showing non-overlapping patterns.\n248\t    MMLU is item-dominated, GSM8K is interaction-dominated, ARC is item+prompt, HellaSwag\n249\t    is prompt+residual.\n250\t  - 'Separability violation heatmap: benchmarks × interaction terms, with cells colored\n251\t    by magnitude. Clear that separability is benchmark-conditional.'\n252\t  analysis: 'The gradient narrative is dead. Variance structure is idiosyncratic per\n253\t    benchmark and not predictable from task type alone. This is actually a finding\n254\t    — \"variance structure is benchmark-specific\" — but it is a negative finding relative\n255\t    to the original framing. Separability fails on 2 benchmarks under binary scoring,\n256\t    undermining the confirmatory null that was supposed to validate isolated-factor\n257\t    studies. The paper must pivot: instead of \"here''s a gradient that explains everything,\"\n258\t    the message becomes \"nothing is simple, everything interacts, and you cannot generalize\n259\t    reproducibility findings across benchmarks.\"\n260\t\n261\t    '\n262\t- name: exp-003 Multi-Hardware G-Study\n263\t  plan_id: exp-003\n264\t  description: 'Multi-GPU type crossing to test hardware × methodology interaction.\n265\t    In the worst tier, second GPU type is unavailable (Manager cannot allocate datacenter\n266\t    GPU within timeline). Fallback: run on westd-32570 only with 2 GPU instances (same\n267\t    architecture) as pseudo-replication. Limited to same RTX PRO 6000 hardware.\n268\t\n269\t    '\n270\t  expected_results:\n271\t    hardware_types_obtained: 1\n272\t    second_gpu_available: false\n273\t    fallback_design: 2 instances of RTX PRO 6000 (same arch, different physical units)\n274\t    gpu_instance_x_method_interaction_pct: 0.08\n275\t    gpu_instance_x_item_pct: 0.03\n276\t    alt_second_gpu_type: A100-80GB (if obtained)\n277\t    alt_gpu_x_method_binary_pct: 2.4\n278\t    alt_gpu_x_prompt_pct: 1.8\n279\t    alt_gpu_x_temperature_pct: 0.9\n280\t    alt_gpu_x_item_pct: 3.1\n281\t    total_records_fallback: 777600\n282\t    experiment_status: degraded — same-architecture pseudo-replication only\n283\t  result_rationale: 'In the worst tier, exp-003 fails its primary objective. Two scenarios:\n284\t    (A) Hardware unavailable: Manager cannot allocate a second GPU architecture within\n285\t    the AAAI timeline. The fallback pseudo-replication (two physical units of RTX\n286\t    PRO 6000) shows near-zero cross-unit variance (0.08%), which is expected — same\n287\t    architecture, same drivers, same CUDA version. This tells us nothing about true\n288\t    hardware×methodology interaction. The \"infrastructure\" facet remains limited to\n289\t    \"numerical precision on a single GPU architecture,\" severely weakening Claim 2.\n290\t    (B) Hardware obtained but interaction is large: if an A100 is obtained, the GPU×method\n291\t    interaction reaches 2.4% under binary scoring (driven by different CUDA kernel\n292\t    implementations for attention between Ampere and Ada Lovelace). GPU×item interaction\n293\t    is even larger (3.1%), meaning hardware affects items differentially — some items\n294\t    are hardware-sensitive while others are not. This breaks the \"precision only\"\n295\t    scoping assumption and means the paper must either include hardware as a full\n296\t    facet (requiring much more analysis) or explicitly scope the study to single-architecture\n297\t    inference. Either outcome damages the paper.\n298\t\n299\t    '\n300\t  tables:\n301\t  - name: 'Table 7a (fallback): Same-Architecture GPU Pseudo-Replication'\n302\t    description: 'Variance decomposition showing gpu_instance contributes <0.1% across\n303\t      all interactions. Demonstrates within-architecture reproducibility is high but\n304\t      says nothing about cross-architecture effects. Footnote: \"True hardware crossing\n305\t      requires heterogeneous GPU architectures; this analysis uses same-architecture\n306\t      replication only.\"\n307\t\n308\t      '\n309\t  - name: 'Table 7b (alt): Cross-Architecture Variance Decomposition (if A100 obtained)'\n310\t    description: 'GPU type as additional facet. GPU×prompt 1.8%, GPU×temperature 0.9%,\n311\t      GPU×item 3.1%. All exceed the 1% threshold used in MVP. Separability assumption\n312\t      for hardware is violated.\n313\t\n314\t      '\n315\t  figures_description:\n316\t  - 'If fallback: simple bar chart showing negligible cross-unit variance. Visually\n317\t    uninteresting but methodologically important as a negative result.'\n318\t  - 'If alt: item-level accuracy difference (A100 minus RTX PRO) distribution, showing\n319\t    a fat-tailed distribution where ~15% of items differ by >5pp across GPU types.'\n320\t  analysis: 'exp-003 either provides no useful data (fallback) or actively harmful\n321\t    data (alt). The fallback forces explicit scoping to single-architecture, which\n322\t    reviewers will flag. The alt scenario compounds the worst-tier narrative by adding\n323\t    yet another interacting facet. Either way, Claim 2 (separability) is damaged.\n324\t    The paper must reframe \"infrastructure\" as \"numerical precision\" only and acknowledge\n325\t    hardware as an uncontrolled confound.\n326\t\n327\t    '\n328\t- name: exp-004 Comprehensive Analysis\n329\t  plan_id: exp-004\n330\t  description: 'Combined analysis of exp-001/001b/002/003 data. This is where the\n331\t    worst-tier narrative crystallizes. Model-as-facet reanalysis, bootstrap CIs, D-study\n332\t    projections, and all methodological defense analyses.\n333\t\n334\t    '\n335\t  expected_results:\n336\t    item_id_pct_mmlu_model_as_facet: 0.224\n337\t    model_pct_mmlu: 0.068\n338\t    model_x_item_pct_mmlu: 0.312\n339\t    prompt_x_item_pct_mmlu_model_facet: 0.048\n340\t    temperature_x_item_pct_mmlu_model_facet: 0.052\n341\t    residual_pct_mmlu_model_facet: 0.119\n342\t    item_id_drop_from_single_to_facet_pp: 33.6\n343\t    model_x_item_is_largest_source: true\n344\t    item_id_pct_gsm8k_model_as_facet: 0.091\n345\t    model_x_item_pct_gsm8k: 0.287\n346\t    seed_x_item_pct_gsm8k_model_facet: 0.112\n347\t    bootstrap_ci_item_id_mmlu_model_facet:\n348\t    - 0.148\n349\t    - 0.301\n350\t    bootstrap_ci_model_x_item_mmlu:\n351\t    - 0.238\n352\t    - 0.387\n353\t    bootstrap_ci_width_item_id_pp: 15.3\n354\t    bootstrap_ci_width_model_x_item_pp: 14.9\n355\t    bootstrap_ci_overlap_item_id_model_x_item: true\n356\t    bootstrap_ci_item_id_gsm8k:\n357\t    - 0.041\n358\t    - 0.142\n359\t    bootstrap_ci_model_x_item_gsm8k:\n360\t    - 0.209\n361\t    - 0.364\n362\t    d_study_items_for_g90_mmlu_binary: 1400\n363\t    d_study_items_for_g90_gsm8k_binary: 2000\n364\t    d_study_items_for_g90_arc_binary: 980\n365\t    d_study_items_for_g90_hellaswag_binary: 1200\n366\t    d_study_items_for_g90_mmlu_text_match: 45\n367\t    d_study_range_across_benchmarks: 45–2000 items\n368\t    d_study_prompts_for_g90_mmlu_binary: 8\n369\t    d_study_seeds_for_g90_mmlu_binary: 4\n370\t    d_study_budget_mmlu_total_conditions: 44800\n371\t    reml_vs_henderson_max_diff_pp: 3.8\n372\t    reml_vs_henderson_rank_order_preserved: false\n373\t    g_coeff_logprob_mmlu: 0.412\n374\t    logprob_item_id_pct: 0.483\n375\t    logprob_model_x_item_pct: 0.198\n376\t    precision_x_temp_alters_rankings: true\n377\t    ranking_instability_fraction: 0.22\n378\t    three_way_interactions_total_pct: 0.042\n379\t    irt_recommended_items_mmlu: 110\n380\t    gtheory_recommended_items_mmlu: 1400\n381\t    ratio_gtheory_to_irt: 12.7\n382\t    min_detectable_interaction_pct: 1.2\n383\t    power_for_2pct_interaction: 0.89\n384\t    fixed_vs_random_max_diff_pp: 7.2\n385\t    temperature_fixed_changes_item_id_by_pp: 4.1\n386\t    bernoulli_variance_correlation_with_item_id: 0.83\n387\t    mechanical_share_of_item_id: 0.61\n388\t    bootstrap_jackknife_ci_ratio: 1.34\n389\t    jackknife_ci_width_item_id_pp: 11.4\n390\t    mmlu_3x3_vs_2x2_item_id_diff_pp: 4.7\n391\t    mmlu_3x3_vs_2x2_changes_rank_order: false\n392\t    g_coeff_binary_mmlu_14k_items_projected: 0.891\n393\t    g_coeff_binary_mmlu_14k_items_ci:\n394\t    - 0.847\n395\t    - 0.934\n396\t    compute_cost_recommended_budget_mmlu_usd: 4200\n397\t    compute_cost_recommended_budget_gsm8k_usd: 11500\n398\t  result_rationale: 'The model-as-facet reanalysis is the earthquake. When model is\n399\t    added as a crossed facet to the MMLU G-study, item_id drops from ~55.8% (single-model)\n400\t    to 22.4%, while model×item interaction emerges at 31.2% — the LARGEST single variance\n401\t    source. This happens because different models find different items difficult:\n402\t    an item that Llama gets right 95% of the time, Mistral may get right only 40%\n403\t    of the time. This differential item functioning (DIF) was hidden in single-model\n404\t    analyses where it was confounded with the residual. The 33.6pp drop in item_id\n405\t    is dramatic and changes the entire paper narrative.\n406\t\n407\t    Bootstrap CIs are wide (15.3pp for item_id, 14.9pp for model×item) and the two\n408\t    CIs overlap, meaning we cannot even definitively say which is larger. This is\n409\t    driven by item-level resampling with only 200 items — each bootstrap resample\n410\t    has ~127 unique items (Euler''s formula), and the item×model interaction is sensitive\n411\t    to which items are included. The width exceeds 12pp on all benchmarks, making\n412\t    confident rank-ordering of variance sources impossible on most benchmarks.\n413\t\n414\t    D-study projections are dire: achieving G≥0.90 under binary scoring requires 1400\n415\t    items on MMLU, 2000 on GSM8K (practically unreachable — GSM8K only has 1319 test\n416\t    items), and 980-1200 on ARC/HellaSwag. These budgets are 3-10× larger than MVP\n417\t    estimates because model×item interaction adds a variance source that cannot be\n418\t    reduced by prompt/seed averaging — only item expansion helps. The cross-benchmark\n419\t    range (45-2000 items) is so large that no single recommendation is actionable.\n420\t\n421\t    The G-theory vs IRT comparison reveals a 12.7× discrepancy (tinyBenchmarks: 110\n422\t    items; G-theory: 1400 items). This is because IRT ignores prompt/seed/temperature/\n423\t    ordering facets entirely — it assumes a single fixed evaluation protocol. The\n424\t    G-theory budget accounts for reproducibility across ALL facets, making it much\n425\t    larger. This is actually a strong finding: IRT-based subsampling produces reliable\n426\t    rankings only within a fixed protocol, not across protocols.\n427\t\n428\t    Fixed vs random sensitivity analysis (MD2) shows a 7.2pp maximum change when temperature\n429\t    is treated as fixed vs random. Temperature has only 2-3 design points, making\n430\t    the random assumption dubious. But the fixed treatment changes item_id by 4.1pp,\n431\t    which is large enough to affect conclusions. This is a genuine methodological\n432\t    vulnerability with no clean resolution.\n433\t\n434\t    The Bernoulli correlation analysis (MD1) confirms that 61% of item_id''s magnitude\n435\t    is mechanically driven by item difficulty spread through p(1-p). This is damning:\n436\t    more than half of the \"headline finding\" (item dominance) is a statistical artifact\n437\t    of using binary scoring on items with heterogeneous difficulty. The text_match\n438\t    metric does not suffer from this, but text_match has different (and less dramatic)\n439\t    variance structure.\n440\t\n441\t    Even projecting to 14K items (full MMLU), binary G reaches only 0.891 [0.847,\n442\t    0.934], still below 0.90 with a CI that includes values as low as 0.847. This\n443\t    means even full MMLU may not provide reliable binary-scored evaluation when considering\n444\t    all facets — a provocative but hard-to-communicate finding.\n445\t\n446\t    '\n447\t  tables:\n448\t  - name: 'Table 8: Model-as-Facet Variance Decomposition (MMLU, binary scoring)'\n449\t    description: 'The central table of the paper. Rows: item_id (22.4%), model (6.8%),\n450\t      model×item (31.2%), prompt×item (4.8%), temp×item (5.2%), precision×item (2.1%),\n451\t      seed (0.4%), prompt (1.2%), temperature (2.8%), precision (4.3%), 3-way+ interactions\n452\t      (4.2%), residual (11.9%). Model×item is largest. Bold. Adjacent column: bootstrap\n453\t      95% CI for each. CIs on item_id and model×item overlap.\n454\t\n455\t      '\n456\t  - name: 'Table 9: Model-as-Facet Variance Decomposition Across Benchmarks'\n457\t    description: 'Same format as Table 8 but columns = MMLU, GSM8K, ARC, HellaSwag.\n458\t      Model×item is largest or co-largest on MMLU (31.2%) and GSM8K (28.7%). On ARC,\n459\t      item_id remains largest (34.1%) with model×item at 22.3%. HellaSwag: prompt×item\n460\t      (18.9%) competes with model×item (21.7%). No consistent pattern across benchmarks.\n461\t\n462\t      '\n463\t  - name: 'Table 10: D-Study Projections for G≥0.90 (Binary Scoring)'\n464\t    description: 'Rows: MMLU, GSM8K, ARC, HellaSwag. Columns: required items, prompts,\n465\t      seeds, total conditions, estimated GPU-hours, USD cost. Items range 980-2000.\n466\t      GSM8K marked \"exceeds available test items.\" Total costs: $4.2K-$11.5K per benchmark.\n467\t\n468\t      '\n469\t  - name: 'Table 11: G-theory vs IRT Item Recommendations'\n470\t    description: 'Comparison table. IRT (tinyBenchmarks): 110 items. G-theory: 1400\n471\t      items. Ratio: 12.7×. Explanation: IRT assumes fixed protocol; G-theory targets\n472\t      cross-protocol reliability. Both are \"correct\" for different reliability definitions.\n473\t\n474\t      '\n475\t  - name: 'Table 12: Bootstrap CI Widths by Variance Source and Benchmark'\n476\t    description: 'All variance sources × all benchmarks. CI widths range 3-16pp. Sources\n477\t      with CI >12pp highlighted. Item_id and model×item exceed 12pp on all benchmarks.\n478\t      Precision main effect CIs are tighter (3-5pp). Message: only main effects can\n479\t      be estimated precisely; interactions and item-dependent sources are noisy.\n480\t\n481\t      '\n482\t  - name: 'Table 13: Methodological Sensitivity Analyses Summary'\n483\t    description: 'Rows: REML vs Henderson, Fixed vs Random, Binary vs TextMatch, 3×3\n484\t      vs 2×2, Bootstrap vs Jackknife. Columns: max difference (pp), rank order preserved\n485\t      (Y/N), conclusion. Two of five analyses show >5pp sensitivity. Overall: results\n486\t      are moderately robust but not insensitive to analytic choices.\n487\t\n488\t      '\n489\t  figures_description:\n490\t  - 'Figure 3 (SIGNATURE): Stacked-bar variance decomposition, single-model vs model-as-facet,\n491\t    side by side for MMLU. Dramatic visual: item_id shrinks, model×item emerges as\n492\t    largest block. The ''before and after'' is the paper''s most striking visual.'\n493\t  - 'Figure 4: Stacked-bar variance decomposition across all 4 benchmarks (model-as-facet).\n494\t    Each bar has a unique ''fingerprint.'' No gradient visible.'\n495\t  - 'Figure 5: D-study projection curves — G vs item count for 4 benchmarks. Curves\n496\t    diverge wildly. GSM8K never reaches G=0.90 within available items. MMLU reaches\n497\t    0.90 at ~1400 items.'\n498\t  - 'Figure 6: Bootstrap CI forest plot for top-5 variance sources on MMLU. Wide,\n499\t    overlapping CIs for item_id and model×item. Tight CIs for precision main effect.'\n500\t  - 'Figure 7: Bernoulli p(1-p) vs observed item variance scatter plot. r=0.83 correlation.\n501\t    Regression line shows 61% of item_id is mechanical.'\n502\t  analysis: 'exp-004 reveals that the entire single-model analysis framework was misleading.\n503\t    Model×item interaction (31.2%) — not item difficulty — is the dominant variance\n504\t    source when models are properly crossed. This is the worst-tier''s central finding\n505\t    and it is genuinely novel and surprising. However, the wide bootstrap CIs, the\n506\t    Bernoulli mechanical artifact, and the benchmark-idiosyncratic patterns make the\n507\t    paper very hard to write cleanly. Every claim requires heavy qualification. The\n508\t    D-study budgets are impractically large and benchmark-specific, providing no actionable\n509\t    \"recipe.\" The G-theory vs IRT comparison is the strongest standalone contribution\n510\t    but is a methods comparison, not an empirical finding. The narrative must shift\n511\t    to: \"evaluation is harder than you think, and here''s exactly why.\"\n512\t\n513\t    '\n514\t- name: exp-005 14B Model Extension\n515\t  plan_id: exp-005\n516\t  description: 'Qwen3-14B and DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B at BF16/FP16. Tests scale effects.\n517\t    Depends on exp-003 (hardware). In worst tier, hardware issues cascade.\n518\t\n519\t    '\n520\t  expected_results:\n521\t    hardware_obtained: false\n522\t    experiment_status: blocked — requires ≥48GB VRAM datacenter GPU not available\n523\t    alt_single_gpu_status: attempted on westd-32570 (96GB) with reduced design\n524\t    alt_total_records: 28800\n525\t    alt_qwen14b_accuracy: 0.738\n526\t    alt_deepseek_r1_distill_accuracy: 0.712\n527\t    alt_item_id_pct_qwen14b: 0.501\n528\t    alt_item_id_pct_deepseek_r1: 0.423\n529\t    alt_cross_scale_item_correlation: 0.64\n530\t    alt_model_x_item_estimated: not estimable with 2-model design\n531\t  result_rationale: 'exp-005 is either blocked entirely (no datacenter GPU) or runs\n532\t    on the single available GPU with a severely reduced design. Even if it runs, 2\n533\t    models is insufficient to estimate model×item interaction as a random effect —\n534\t    you need ≥3 for meaningful variance component estimation. The cross-scale item\n535\t    difficulty correlation (r=0.64 between 8B and 14B models) is lower than within-scale\n536\t    correlation (r=0.71-0.78 among 8B models), suggesting that scale increases model×item\n537\t    interaction further. This is consistent with the worst-tier narrative (larger\n538\t    models have more distinct item-level competence profiles) but the evidence is\n539\t    too thin to make claims. The experiment contributes almost nothing to the paper\n540\t    in the worst tier.\n541\t\n542\t    '\n543\t  tables:\n544\t  - name: 'Table A1 (Appendix): 14B Model Accuracy Summary'\n545\t    description: 'Minimal 2×2 table (2 models × 2 precisions). Qwen3-14B: 73.8%, DeepSeek-R1-Distill:\n546\t      71.2%. Noted as \"reduced design, insufficient for G-theory analysis.\"\n547\t\n548\t      '\n549\t  figures_description:\n550\t  - 'Scatter plot: 8B vs 14B item-level accuracy. r=0.64. Points dispersed more than\n551\t    within-scale comparisons. Suggestive but underpowered.'\n552\t  analysis: 'exp-005 is the clearest casualty of the worst tier. It either doesn''t\n553\t    run or produces too little data to support claims. Relegated to a brief appendix\n554\t    mention or dropped entirely. The cross-scale correlation finding (r=0.64) is interesting\n555\t    but requires follow-up work with more models and a full crossing design.\n556\t\n557\t    '\n558\t- name: exp-006 Fine-Tuning G-Study\n559\t  plan_id: exp-006\n560\t  description: 'RoBERTa-large on SST-2/MNLI/RTE/COPA. 6 seeds × 3 learning rates ×\n561\t    4 data shuffles × 3 GPUs × 2 precisions = 432 runs/task × 4 tasks = 1,728 total\n562\t    runs. Independent of inference experiments. Tests fine-tuning variance hierarchy.\n563\t\n564\t    '\n565\t  expected_results:\n566\t    total_runs_completed: 1614\n567\t    total_runs_planned: 1728\n568\t    failure_rate: 0.066\n569\t    failed_config: lr=5e-4 × fp16 on RTE/COPA (small datasets, unstable)\n570\t    item_id_pct_sst2: 0.312\n571\t    seed_pct_sst2: 0.089\n572\t    lr_pct_sst2: 0.143\n573\t    data_shuffle_pct_sst2: 0.034\n574\t    gpu_pct_sst2: 0.002\n575\t    precision_pct_sst2: 0.018\n576\t    seed_x_lr_pct_sst2: 0.112\n577\t    residual_pct_sst2: 0.178\n578\t    g_coeff_sst2: 0.487\n579\t    item_id_pct_mnli: 0.401\n580\t    lr_pct_mnli: 0.098\n581\t    seed_pct_mnli: 0.067\n582\t    seed_x_item_pct_mnli: 0.134\n583\t    residual_pct_mnli: 0.156\n584\t    g_coeff_mnli: 0.534\n585\t    item_id_pct_rte: 0.187\n586\t    seed_pct_rte: 0.203\n587\t    lr_pct_rte: 0.178\n588\t    seed_x_lr_pct_rte: 0.156\n589\t    residual_pct_rte: 0.214\n590\t    g_coeff_rte: 0.298\n591\t    item_id_pct_copa: 0.134\n592\t    seed_pct_copa: 0.241\n593\t    lr_pct_copa: 0.198\n594\t    residual_pct_copa: 0.267\n595\t    g_coeff_copa: 0.213\n596\t    inference_vs_finetuning_rank_correlation: 0.31\n597\t    dominant_source_inference: item_id (or model×item)\n598\t    dominant_source_finetuning: item_id on large datasets; seed on small datasets\n599\t  result_rationale: 'Fine-tuning variance hierarchy differs substantially from inference.\n600\t    On large datasets (SST-2, MNLI), item_id is still the largest source (31-40%)\n601\t    but learning rate and seed×learning_rate interactions are substantial (10-14%),\n602\t    which have no analogue in inference. On small datasets (RTE: 277 items; COPA:\n603\t    100 items), seed becomes dominant (20-24%) because the training dynamics are chaotic\n604\t    with few examples — small perturbations in initialization cascade through training.\n605\t\n606\t    GPU and precision contribute negligibly to fine-tuning variance (<2% combined),\n607\t    which contrasts with the 4-7% precision contribution in inference. This is because\n608\t    fine-tuning aggregates over many gradient steps, averaging out per-forward-pass\n609\t    precision effects.\n610\t\n611\t    The cross-paradigm rank correlation (r=0.31) between inference and fine-tuning\n612\t    variance hierarchies means the findings do NOT transfer. A reviewer cannot extrapolate\n613\t    from our inference G-study to fine-tuning practice, and we cannot extrapolate\n614\t    from exp-006 back to inference. This supports the worst-tier narrative (\"nothing\n615\t    generalizes\") but also means exp-006 is somewhat disconnected from the paper''s\n616\t    main story. It becomes an appendix-level extension rather than a core contribution.\n617\t\n618\t    The 6.6% run failure rate (NaN loss on high-lr×fp16 combinations with small datasets)\n619\t    is a methodological nuisance. These failures are informative (they show that some\n620\t    precision×hyperparameter combinations are unstable) but create missing cells in\n621\t    the G-theory design, requiring imputation or reduced analysis.\n622\t\n623\t    '\n624\t  tables:\n625\t  - name: 'Table 14: Fine-Tuning Variance Decomposition Across Tasks'\n626\t    description: 'Rows: seed, learning_rate, data_shuffle, gpu, precision, item_id,\n627\t      seed×lr, seed×item, residual. Columns: SST-2, MNLI, RTE, COPA. Clear split between\n628\t      large-dataset (item-dominated) and small-dataset (seed-dominated) patterns.\n629\t\n630\t      '\n631\t  - name: 'Table 15: Inference vs Fine-Tuning Variance Hierarchy Comparison'\n632\t    description: 'Side-by-side ranking of variance sources. Inference: item_id > model×item\n633\t      > residual > temperature×item > prompt×item. Fine-tuning: item_id > seed×lr\n634\t      > seed > lr > residual. Different hierarchies, different implications.\n635\t\n636\t      '\n637\t  figures_description:\n638\t  - 'Paired stacked-bar: inference (MMLU) vs fine-tuning (SST-2) variance decomposition.\n639\t    Visually distinct patterns — different colors dominate each bar.'\n640\t  - 'G-coefficient comparison: inference tasks vs fine-tuning tasks. Fine-tuning G\n641\t    is generally higher on large datasets but lower on small datasets.'\n642\t  analysis: 'exp-006 produces a valid standalone result (fine-tuning variance hierarchy\n643\t    differs from inference) but its contribution to the main paper is ambiguous. It\n644\t    supports the worst-tier message (\"nothing generalizes across paradigms\") but also\n645\t    weakens coherence — the paper is already struggling to find a unifying narrative,\n646\t    and adding a disconnected fine-tuning analysis makes it more scattered. Recommend\n647\t    relegating to a 1-page appendix or splitting into a separate short paper.\n648\t\n649\t    '\n650\t- name: exp-007 LayerCast Precision Mitigation\n651\t  plan_id: exp-007\n652\t  description: 'LayerCast (FP16 storage + FP32 compute) as additional precision condition\n653\t    on MMLU. Tests whether this mixed-precision strategy reduces variance from the\n654\t    precision facet.\n655\t\n656\t    '\n657\t  expected_results:\n658\t    total_records: 43200\n659\t    layercast_accuracy: 0.6482\n660\t    layercast_vs_fp32_gap_pp: 4.88\n661\t    layercast_vs_bf16_gap_pp: -3.62\n662\t    precision_variance_with_layercast_pct: 5.1\n663\t    layercast_item_level_correlation_with_bf16: 0.87\n664\t    layercast_item_level_correlation_with_fp32: 0.72\n665\t    variance_reduction_from_layercast: none — adds variance rather than reducing it\n666\t  result_rationale: 'LayerCast does not cleanly reduce variance because it creates\n667\t    a hybrid precision regime that behaves differently from both pure FP32 and pure\n668\t    FP16/BF16. The storage-in-FP16, compute-in-FP32 approach means attention computations\n669\t    use FP32 but weight quantization noise from FP16 storage propagates through. The\n670\t    result is an accuracy level between FP32 and BF16 (64.8%), but with item-level\n671\t    patterns that don''t correlate perfectly with either pure precision. Adding LayerCast\n672\t    as a 4th precision level actually INCREASES precision facet variance (from 4.3%\n673\t    to 5.1%) because it adds another distinct level rather than collapsing precision\n674\t    differences. This is a negative result for the mitigation narrative but a positive\n675\t    result for the worst-tier message: precision effects are complex and non-monotonic,\n676\t    and simple mitigation strategies don''t work as advertised.\n677\t\n678\t    '\n679\t  tables:\n680\t  - name: 'Table A2 (Appendix): LayerCast Variance Impact'\n681\t    description: '4-row precision comparison (FP32, FP16, BF16, LayerCast) with accuracy,\n682\t      item-level correlation with BF16, and precision facet variance contribution.\n683\t      LayerCast is intermediate but not a clean fix.\n684\t\n685\t      '\n686\t  figures_description:\n687\t  - 'Item-level accuracy scatter: LayerCast vs BF16 (r=0.87) and LayerCast vs FP32\n688\t    (r=0.72). Two panels showing LayerCast is closer to BF16 but not a drop-in replacement.'\n689\t  analysis: 'exp-007 is a minor negative result. LayerCast doesn''t serve as a precision\n690\t    mitigation strategy in this context. Useful as a 1-paragraph appendix note showing\n691\t    that mixed-precision approaches don''t simplify the variance picture. Low priority\n692\t    for inclusion in the worst-tier paper.\n693\t\n694\t    '\n695\t- name: exp-008 MATH Benchmark Extension\n696\t  plan_id: exp-008\n697\t  description: 'MATH benchmark (200 items) with chain-of-thought reasoning. Tests\n698\t    whether reasoning-heavy tasks with long generation have qualitatively different\n699\t    variance structure.\n700\t\n701\t    '\n702\t  expected_results:\n703\t    total_records: 115200\n704\t    models_completed: 2\n705\t    overall_accuracy_llama: 0.341\n706\t    overall_accuracy_qwen: 0.428\n707\t    accuracy_range_across_conditions: 0.187\n708\t    item_id_pct_math: 0.118\n709\t    seed_pct_math: 0.134\n710\t    temperature_pct_math: 0.156\n711\t    prompt_pct_math: 0.087\n712\t    seed_x_item_pct_math: 0.168\n713\t    temperature_x_item_pct_math: 0.143\n714\t    precision_x_temperature_pct_math_binary: 4.7\n715\t    residual_pct_math: 0.194\n716\t    g_coeff_binary_math_200item: 0.152\n717\t    d_study_items_for_g90_math_binary: not achievable within MATH test set size\n718\t    avg_generation_length_tokens: 487\n719\t    generation_length_cv: 0.68\n720\t    accuracy_conditional_on_cot_length: non-monotonic — medium length CoT has highest\n721\t      accuracy\n722\t  result_rationale: 'MATH represents the extreme end of the worst-tier pattern. Item_id\n723\t    is only 11.8% — the lowest of any benchmark — because chain-of-thought generation\n724\t    introduces massive stochastic branching. Each reasoning step is a decision point\n725\t    where temperature and seed can send the model down different solution paths. The\n726\t    seed×item (16.8%) and temperature×item (14.3%) interactions are the dominant sources,\n727\t    meaning the same item can be solved or not depending on the random seed and temperature\n728\t    — an extreme form of the model×item interaction pattern.\n729\t\n730\t    The 18.7pp accuracy range across conditions for the same model on the same items\n731\t    is striking: the \"same\" evaluation can produce 34.1% or 52.8% accuracy for Llama\n732\t    on MATH depending on prompt/seed/temperature. This makes any single-run MATH evaluation\n733\t    essentially meaningless for fine-grained model comparison.\n734\t\n735\t    Separability fails catastrophically: precision×temperature interaction is 4.7%\n736\t    under binary scoring, the highest of any benchmark. This is because FP16/BF16\n737\t    rounding in the softmax creates different token selections at each reasoning step,\n738\t    and at higher temperatures these divergences compound over 400+ tokens of chain-of-thought.\n739\t\n740\t    G=0.152 for 200 items under binary scoring is the lowest across all benchmarks.\n741\t    D-study projections show G≥0.90 is not achievable within MATH''s test set (5000\n742\t    items) because the dominant variance sources (seed×item, temperature×item) cannot\n743\t    be reduced by adding items — they require averaging over many seeds and temperatures\n744\t    per item, which is computationally expensive.\n745\t\n746\t    Mistral''s exclusion (accuracy <10%, effectively random guessing on MATH) further\n747\t    reduces model coverage to 2, making model-as-facet analysis impossible on this\n748\t    benchmark.\n749\t\n750\t    '\n751\t  tables:\n752\t  - name: 'Table A3 (Appendix): MATH Variance Decomposition'\n753\t    description: 'Seed×item (16.8%) and temperature×item (14.3%) dominate. Item_id\n754\t      is only 11.8%. Precision×temperature is 4.7%. G=0.152. The most extreme interaction-dominated\n755\t      pattern in the study.\n756\t\n757\t      '\n758\t  - name: 'Table A4: Accuracy Range by Model Across Conditions (MATH)'\n759\t    description: '2 models × min/max/range across all conditions. Llama: 22.5-52.8%\n760\t      (range 30.3pp). Qwen: 31.2-58.4% (range 27.2pp). Demonstrates that single-run\n761\t      MATH evaluation is unreliable.\n762\t\n763\t      '\n764\t  figures_description:\n765\t  - 'Heatmap: seed × temperature accuracy grid for Llama on MATH. High variance visible\n766\t    — hot spots and cold spots across the grid with no smooth pattern.'\n767\t  - 'Chain-of-thought length distribution by accuracy outcome (correct vs incorrect).\n768\t    Bimodal: very short (gave up) and very long (went in circles) both predict failure.'\n769\t  analysis: 'MATH is the poster child for the worst-tier narrative: everything interacts,\n770\t    nothing is stable, and single-run evaluation is meaningless. But with only 2 models\n771\t    and a reduced design, the evidence is thin. The extreme interaction dominance\n772\t    is compelling but also predictable (CoT reasoning is inherently stochastic). The\n773\t    finding reinforces the \"no gradient\" message — MATH is not simply \"one step further\"\n774\t    on a knowledge→reasoning continuum; it has a qualitatively different variance\n775\t    structure that shares nothing with MMLU or ARC. Best used as a dramatic closing\n776\t    example in the paper rather than a core analysis.'\n777\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"89b1a144-a44f-4cdf-8aa3-cf1e3e3d89c1","uuid":"7f6491b0-2295-4dfb-9ec1-6b6e988b3ee2","timestamp":"2026-05-21T04:33:22.238Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519/projects/nlp_variance_gtheory/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T032813/worst/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'Core framing (\"item dominance + benchmark gradient\") collapses.\n  Reframe around: \"Model×item interaction is the hidden dominant variance source in\n  LLM evaluation — items that are easy for one model are hard for another, and this\n  interaction, not item difficulty alone, drives irreproducibility.\" Separability\n  failure becomes a positive finding: isolated-factor studies are insufficient. D-study\n  pivots to \"current evaluation practices are even more inadequate than prior work\n  suggests.\" Drop the gradient narrative entirely; replace with \"variance structure\n  is benchmark-specific and unpredictable from task taxonomy alone.\" The paper becomes\n  more iconoclastic. ACL ARR October becomes realistic primary target.\n\n  '\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: exp-001 Full-Scale Precision Study (MMLU)\n  plan_id: exp-001\n  description: '4 models × 3 precisions × 6 prompts × 6 seeds × 3 temperatures × 4\n    orderings × 200 MMLU items. Gemma-2-9B-IT fails during FP16 runs (known from registry:\n    exp001_gemma FAILED). Qwen3-8B produces sporadic decoding anomalies under FP32\n    at T=0.7, requiring partial reruns. Final dataset covers 3 models (Llama, Mistral,\n    Qwen) with Gemma fully excluded, yielding 3 × 1296 = 3888 conditions × 200 items\n    = 777,600 records.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    total_records: 777600\n    models_completed: 3\n    models_excluded: 1\n    conditions_per_model: 1296\n    items_per_condition: 200\n    overall_accuracy_llama: 0.681\n    overall_accuracy_mistral: 0.623\n    overall_accuracy_qwen: 0.704\n    accuracy_bfloat16_pooled: 0.68\n    accuracy_float16_pooled: 0.678\n    accuracy_float32_pooled: 0.611\n    float32_degradation_pp: 6.8\n    null_predictions_pct: 0.03\n    item_id_pct_llama_single: 0.561\n    item_id_pct_mistral_single: 0.534\n    item_id_pct_qwen_single: 0.589\n    residual_pct_llama_single: 0.182\n    residual_pct_mistral_single: 0.197\n    residual_pct_qwen_single: 0.168\n    g_coeff_binary_llama_200item: 0.362\n    g_coeff_binary_mistral_200item: 0.338\n    g_coeff_binary_qwen_200item: 0.371\n    g_coeff_text_match_llama_200item: 0.968\n    precision_x_temperature_text_match_llama_pct: 1.5\n    precision_x_temperature_text_match_mistral_pct: 1.8\n  result_rationale: 'Single-model analyses replicate MVP findings: item_id dominates\n    at 53-59% per model, consistent with Bernoulli variance spread across 200 items\n    of heterogeneous difficulty. FP32 degradation (~6.8pp) replicates the V0 enforce_eager-controlled\n    pattern from bf16_eager_disentangle. These per-model results look encouraging\n    — item dominance appears stable. But this is misleading: the residual component\n    (17-20%) is inflated because model×item interaction is confounded with residual\n    when model is not a facet. The true picture emerges only in exp-004 reanalysis.\n    Gemma exclusion reduces the model universe from 4 to 3, weakening generalizability\n    claims and inviting reviewer criticism about restricted model coverage. Qwen FP32\n    anomalies at T=0.7 suggest architecture-specific precision sensitivity, foreshadowing\n    the idiosyncratic variance patterns that dominate the worst-tier narrative.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 1: Single-Model Variance Decomposition (MMLU, 200 items, binary scoring)'\n    description: 'Rows: variance sources (item_id, prompt, seed, temperature, ordering,\n      precision, all 2-way interactions, residual). Columns: Llama, Mistral, Qwen,\n      Pooled. Shows item_id dominant at 53-59% across all 3 models. Residual inflated\n      at 17-20%. Precision×temperature interaction 0.3-0.5% under binary, 1.5-1.8%\n      under text_match.\n\n      '\n  - name: 'Table 2: Accuracy Summary by Precision × Temperature × Model'\n    description: '3×3×3 table. FP32 degradation visible across all models (5.5-8.5pp).\n      Temperature effect modest (T=0.0 vs T=0.7: 2-3pp). Cross-model accuracy spread:\n      8pp.\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - Accuracy heatmap (3 models × 3 precisions × 3 temperatures). FP32 row visibly\n    darker. Model ranking preserved across conditions but gap varies.\n  - 'Item-level accuracy distributions per model: overlapping but offset histograms\n    showing each model has distinct difficulty profile for the same 200 items.'\n  analysis: 'Data collection succeeds for 3/4 models. Per-model G-studies replicate\n    MVP item dominance finding. However, item-level correlation between models is\n    only r=0.71-0.78 (not r=0.90+), foreshadowing large model×item interaction in\n    the facet reanalysis. The single-model framing looks solid at this stage but will\n    collapse in exp-004.\n\n    '\n- name: exp-001b Item Resampling Stability\n  plan_id: exp-001b\n  description: '3 independent stratified 200-item MMLU samples (seeds 0, 7, 99) ×\n    Llama × 3 precisions × 3 prompts × 3 seeds × 2 orderings = 54 conditions per sample.\n    Tests whether item_id dominance percentage is stable across item draws.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    total_records_per_sample: 10800\n    total_records: 32400\n    item_id_pct_sample_seed0: 0.541\n    item_id_pct_sample_seed7: 0.612\n    item_id_pct_sample_seed99: 0.487\n    item_id_range_pp: 12.5\n    residual_pct_range:\n    - 0.155\n    - 0.221\n    g_coeff_binary_seed0: 0.341\n    g_coeff_binary_seed7: 0.389\n    g_coeff_binary_seed99: 0.312\n    g_coeff_range_pp: 7.7\n    item_difficulty_mean_seed0: 0.683\n    item_difficulty_mean_seed7: 0.671\n    item_difficulty_mean_seed99: 0.698\n    item_difficulty_sd_seed0: 0.284\n    item_difficulty_sd_seed7: 0.301\n    item_difficulty_sd_seed99: 0.262\n  result_rationale: 'The 12.5pp range in item_id percentage across three samples (48.7-61.2%)\n    is uncomfortably wide. This is driven by differential item difficulty variance:\n    sample seed99 has lower difficulty SD (0.262 vs 0.301), mechanically reducing\n    Bernoulli p(1-p) spread and thus item_id''s share. This directly validates the\n    MD1 concern — item_id percentage is partly a mechanical artifact of the item difficulty\n    distribution in the sample, not a stable psychometric property. The instability\n    makes it impossible to report a single \"item_id = X%\" headline number with confidence.\n    In the worst tier, this is compounded by the fact that model×item interaction\n    (not measurable in single-model exp-001b) will further erode whatever item_id\n    estimate survives. Bootstrap CIs on item_id will be wide (±6-8pp per sample),\n    and the cross-sample range exceeds the within-sample CI, indicating genuine instability\n    rather than sampling noise alone.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 3: Variance Decomposition Stability Across Item Samples'\n    description: 'Rows: variance sources. Columns: Sample A (seed0), Sample B (seed7),\n      Sample C (seed99), Range. Item_id ranges 48.7-61.2%. Residual ranges 15.5-22.1%.\n      Prompt×item ranges 4.8-7.2%. Instability is systematic, not random.\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - Three-panel stacked bar chart showing variance decomposition for each item sample.\n    Visible structural differences despite identical design and model.\n  - 'Scatter plot: item difficulty SD vs item_id percentage across samples, showing\n    positive correlation (mechanical Bernoulli link).'\n  analysis: 'Item dominance magnitude is sample-dependent, with a 12.5pp range across\n    three draws. This is a serious threat to the headline claim. The Bernoulli variance\n    mechanism (MD1) explains ~60% of the cross-sample variation. Combined with the\n    model×item interaction finding from exp-004, the \"item dominance\" narrative becomes\n    untenable as a clean headline. The paper must reframe: item_id is large but its\n    magnitude is unstable and partly mechanical; the more interesting finding is what\n    happens when model is added as a facet.\n\n    '\n- name: exp-002 Multi-Benchmark Replication\n  plan_id: exp-002\n  description: '3 models (Gemma excluded) × 2 precisions (BF16/FP16) × 6 prompts ×\n    6 seeds × 2 temperatures × 4 orderings × 200 items each on GSM8K, ARC-Challenge,\n    HellaSwag. Reduced crossing (2×2 vs 3×3). Tests cross-benchmark gradient hypothesis.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    total_records_gsm8k: 172800\n    total_records_arc: 172800\n    total_records_hellaswag: 172800\n    models_completed_gsm8k: 3\n    models_completed_arc: 2\n    models_completed_hellaswag: 3\n    benchmarks_with_complete_data: 3\n    item_id_pct_gsm8k_pooled: 0.143\n    seed_pct_gsm8k: 0.089\n    temperature_pct_gsm8k: 0.112\n    prompt_pct_gsm8k: 0.067\n    seed_x_item_pct_gsm8k: 0.138\n    temperature_x_item_pct_gsm8k: 0.124\n    residual_pct_gsm8k: 0.247\n    precision_x_temperature_gsm8k_binary_pct: 3.2\n    g_coeff_binary_gsm8k_200item: 0.198\n    item_id_pct_arc_pooled: 0.472\n    seed_pct_arc: 0.018\n    temperature_pct_arc: 0.031\n    prompt_pct_arc: 0.093\n    residual_pct_arc: 0.196\n    precision_x_temperature_arc_binary_pct: 0.4\n    g_coeff_binary_arc_200item: 0.311\n    item_id_pct_hellaswag_pooled: 0.387\n    seed_pct_hellaswag: 0.042\n    temperature_pct_hellaswag: 0.058\n    prompt_pct_hellaswag: 0.121\n    precision_x_prompt_hellaswag_binary_pct: 3.8\n    residual_pct_hellaswag: 0.203\n    g_coeff_binary_hellaswag_200item: 0.274\n    cross_benchmark_gradient_pp: 5.1\n    item_id_ordering_by_benchmark: ARC(47.2%) > HellaSwag(38.7%) > MMLU(~55%) > GSM8K(14.3%)\n    gradient_correlation_with_task_type: -0.12\n    direct_infra_x_method_binary_gsm8k: 3.2\n    direct_infra_x_method_binary_hellaswag: 3.8\n    direct_infra_x_method_binary_arc: 0.4\n    direct_infra_x_method_text_match_gsm8k: 7.4\n    direct_infra_x_method_text_match_hellaswag: 5.9\n    benchmarks_separability_fails_binary: 2\n  result_rationale: 'The cross-benchmark gradient hypothesis collapses. The item_id\n    ordering is MMLU(55%) > ARC(47%) > HellaSwag(39%) > GSM8K(14%), which does not\n    follow any coherent task taxonomy. ARC (science reasoning) has higher item_id\n    than HellaSwag (commonsense), despite commonsense items being more \"knowledge-like.\"\n    GSM8K''s low item_id (14.3%) is expected — math reasoning items have high seed×item\n    and temperature×item interactions because chain-of-thought generation introduces\n    stochastic branching at each reasoning step. But HellaSwag''s intermediate position\n    and ARC''s high position are not predicted by any task-type theory. The correlation\n    between task-type ranking and item_id ranking is near zero (r=-0.12), destroying\n    the \"knowledge→reasoning gradient\" narrative.\n\n    Separability fails on GSM8K (precision×temperature = 3.2% under binary) and HellaSwag\n    (precision×prompt = 3.8%). The GSM8K failure is interpretable: at T=0.7, floating-point\n    non-associativity in the softmax creates different token selection paths across\n    precisions, and for multi-step reasoning, these divergences compound. The HellaSwag\n    failure is less interpretable — precision×prompt interaction suggests that certain\n    prompt framings expose precision-dependent attention patterns in the commonsense\n    completion task. Neither failure was predicted by the MVP data (which showed separability\n    under binary scoring on MMLU). This means the Claim 2 separability result is benchmark-conditional\n    in a way that undermines confident generalization.\n\n    The residual components are large everywhere (19.6-24.7%), foreshadowing the model×item\n    interaction that will emerge when model is treated as a facet in exp-004.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 4: Variance Decomposition Across Benchmarks (Single-Model Pooled,\n      Binary Scoring)'\n    description: 'Rows: 10 variance sources. Columns: MMLU, GSM8K, ARC-Challenge,\n      HellaSwag. No systematic gradient visible. Item_id ranges from 14.3% (GSM8K)\n      to 55%+ (MMLU). Residual uniformly large (19-25%). Interaction terms vary idiosyncratically.\n\n      '\n  - name: 'Table 5: Infrastructure × Methodology Interactions (Binary + Text Match)'\n    description: 'All 2-way infra×method interactions per benchmark under both scoring\n      metrics. Binary: GSM8K 3.2%, HellaSwag 3.8%, ARC 0.4%, MMLU 0.3%. Text match:\n      GSM8K 7.4%, HellaSwag 5.9%. Separability fails on 2/4 benchmarks under binary.\n\n      '\n  - name: 'Table 6: G-coefficients by Benchmark and Metric'\n    description: 'Binary G: MMLU 0.36, ARC 0.31, HellaSwag 0.27, GSM8K 0.20. Text\n      match G: MMLU 0.97, GSM8K 0.88, ARC 0.91, HellaSwag 0.93. Binary G is poor everywhere;\n      text match is acceptable but variable.\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - Stacked-bar variance decomposition across 4 benchmarks. The signature figure —\n    visually demonstrates NO gradient. Each benchmark has a unique 'fingerprint' of\n    variance sources.\n  - Radar chart of top-5 variance sources per benchmark, showing non-overlapping patterns.\n    MMLU is item-dominated, GSM8K is interaction-dominated, ARC is item+prompt, HellaSwag\n    is prompt+residual.\n  - 'Separability violation heatmap: benchmarks × interaction terms, with cells colored\n    by magnitude. Clear that separability is benchmark-conditional.'\n  analysis: 'The gradient narrative is dead. Variance structure is idiosyncratic per\n    benchmark and not predictable from task type alone. This is actually a finding\n    — \"variance structure is benchmark-specific\" — but it is a negative finding relative\n    to the original framing. Separability fails on 2 benchmarks under binary scoring,\n    undermining the confirmatory null that was supposed to validate isolated-factor\n    studies. The paper must pivot: instead of \"here''s a gradient that explains everything,\"\n    the message becomes \"nothing is simple, everything interacts, and you cannot generalize\n    reproducibility findings across benchmarks.\"\n\n    '\n- name: exp-003 Multi-Hardware G-Study\n  plan_id: exp-003\n  description: 'Multi-GPU type crossing to test hardware × methodology interaction.\n    In the worst tier, second GPU type is unavailable (Manager cannot allocate datacenter\n    GPU within timeline). Fallback: run on westd-32570 only with 2 GPU instances (same\n    architecture) as pseudo-replication. Limited to same RTX PRO 6000 hardware.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    hardware_types_obtained: 1\n    second_gpu_available: false\n    fallback_design: 2 instances of RTX PRO 6000 (same arch, different physical units)\n    gpu_instance_x_method_interaction_pct: 0.08\n    gpu_instance_x_item_pct: 0.03\n    alt_second_gpu_type: A100-80GB (if obtained)\n    alt_gpu_x_method_binary_pct: 2.4\n    alt_gpu_x_prompt_pct: 1.8\n    alt_gpu_x_temperature_pct: 0.9\n    alt_gpu_x_item_pct: 3.1\n    total_records_fallback: 777600\n    experiment_status: degraded — same-architecture pseudo-replication only\n  result_rationale: 'In the worst tier, exp-003 fails its primary objective. Two scenarios:\n    (A) Hardware unavailable: Manager cannot allocate a second GPU architecture within\n    the AAAI timeline. The fallback pseudo-replication (two physical units of RTX\n    PRO 6000) shows near-zero cross-unit variance (0.08%), which is expected — same\n    architecture, same drivers, same CUDA version. This tells us nothing about true\n    hardware×methodology interaction. The \"infrastructure\" facet remains limited to\n    \"numerical precision on a single GPU architecture,\" severely weakening Claim 2.\n    (B) Hardware obtained but interaction is large: if an A100 is obtained, the GPU×method\n    interaction reaches 2.4% under binary scoring (driven by different CUDA kernel\n    implementations for attention between Ampere and Ada Lovelace). GPU×item interaction\n    is even larger (3.1%), meaning hardware affects items differentially — some items\n    are hardware-sensitive while others are not. This breaks the \"precision only\"\n    scoping assumption and means the paper must either include hardware as a full\n    facet (requiring much more analysis) or explicitly scope the study to single-architecture\n    inference. Either outcome damages the paper.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 7a (fallback): Same-Architecture GPU Pseudo-Replication'\n    description: 'Variance decomposition showing gpu_instance contributes <0.1% across\n      all interactions. Demonstrates within-architecture reproducibility is high but\n      says nothing about cross-architecture effects. Footnote: \"True hardware crossing\n      requires heterogeneous GPU architectures; this analysis uses same-architecture\n      replication only.\"\n\n      '\n  - name: 'Table 7b (alt): Cross-Architecture Variance Decomposition (if A100 obtained)'\n    description: 'GPU type as additional facet. GPU×prompt 1.8%, GPU×temperature 0.9%,\n      GPU×item 3.1%. All exceed the 1% threshold used in MVP. Separability assumption\n      for hardware is violated.\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - 'If fallback: simple bar chart showing negligible cross-unit variance. Visually\n    uninteresting but methodologically important as a negative result.'\n  - 'If alt: item-level accuracy difference (A100 minus RTX PRO) distribution, showing\n    a fat-tailed distribution where ~15% of items differ by >5pp across GPU types.'\n  analysis: 'exp-003 either provides no useful data (fallback) or actively harmful\n    data (alt). The fallback forces explicit scoping to single-architecture, which\n    reviewers will flag. The alt scenario compounds the worst-tier narrative by adding\n    yet another interacting facet. Either way, Claim 2 (separability) is damaged.\n    The paper must reframe \"infrastructure\" as \"numerical precision\" only and acknowledge\n    hardware as an uncontrolled confound.\n\n    '\n- name: exp-004 Comprehensive Analysis\n  plan_id: exp-004\n  description: 'Combined analysis of exp-001/001b/002/003 data. This is where the\n    worst-tier narrative crystallizes. Model-as-facet reanalysis, bootstrap CIs, D-study\n    projections, and all methodological defense analyses.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    item_id_pct_mmlu_model_as_facet: 0.224\n    model_pct_mmlu: 0.068\n    model_x_item_pct_mmlu: 0.312\n    prompt_x_item_pct_mmlu_model_facet: 0.048\n    temperature_x_item_pct_mmlu_model_facet: 0.052\n    residual_pct_mmlu_model_facet: 0.119\n    item_id_drop_from_single_to_facet_pp: 33.6\n    model_x_item_is_largest_source: true\n    item_id_pct_gsm8k_model_as_facet: 0.091\n    model_x_item_pct_gsm8k: 0.287\n    seed_x_item_pct_gsm8k_model_facet: 0.112\n    bootstrap_ci_item_id_mmlu_model_facet:\n    - 0.148\n    - 0.301\n    bootstrap_ci_model_x_item_mmlu:\n    - 0.238\n    - 0.387\n    bootstrap_ci_width_item_id_pp: 15.3\n    bootstrap_ci_width_model_x_item_pp: 14.9\n    bootstrap_ci_overlap_item_id_model_x_item: true\n    bootstrap_ci_item_id_gsm8k:\n    - 0.041\n    - 0.142\n    bootstrap_ci_model_x_item_gsm8k:\n    - 0.209\n    - 0.364\n    d_study_items_for_g90_mmlu_binary: 1400\n    d_study_items_for_g90_gsm8k_binary: 2000\n    d_study_items_for_g90_arc_binary: 980\n    d_study_items_for_g90_hellaswag_binary: 1200\n    d_study_items_for_g90_mmlu_text_match: 45\n    d_study_range_across_benchmarks: 45–2000 items\n    d_study_prompts_for_g90_mmlu_binary: 8\n    d_study_seeds_for_g90_mmlu_binary: 4\n    d_study_budget_mmlu_total_conditions: 44800\n    reml_vs_henderson_max_diff_pp: 3.8\n    reml_vs_henderson_rank_order_preserved: false\n    g_coeff_logprob_mmlu: 0.412\n    logprob_item_id_pct: 0.483\n    logprob_model_x_item_pct: 0.198\n    precision_x_temp_alters_rankings: true\n    ranking_instability_fraction: 0.22\n    three_way_interactions_total_pct: 0.042\n    irt_recommended_items_mmlu: 110\n    gtheory_recommended_items_mmlu: 1400\n    ratio_gtheory_to_irt: 12.7\n    min_detectable_interaction_pct: 1.2\n    power_for_2pct_interaction: 0.89\n    fixed_vs_random_max_diff_pp: 7.2\n    temperature_fixed_changes_item_id_by_pp: 4.1\n    bernoulli_variance_correlation_with_item_id: 0.83\n    mechanical_share_of_item_id: 0.61\n    bootstrap_jackknife_ci_ratio: 1.34\n    jackknife_ci_width_item_id_pp: 11.4\n    mmlu_3x3_vs_2x2_item_id_diff_pp: 4.7\n    mmlu_3x3_vs_2x2_changes_rank_order: false\n    g_coeff_binary_mmlu_14k_items_projected: 0.891\n    g_coeff_binary_mmlu_14k_items_ci:\n    - 0.847\n    - 0.934\n    compute_cost_recommended_budget_mmlu_usd: 4200\n    compute_cost_recommended_budget_gsm8k_usd: 11500\n  result_rationale: 'The model-as-facet reanalysis is the earthquake. When model is\n    added as a crossed facet to the MMLU G-study, item_id drops from ~55.8% (single-model)\n    to 22.4%, while model×item interaction emerges at 31.2% — the LARGEST single variance\n    source. This happens because different models find different items difficult:\n    an item that Llama gets right 95% of the time, Mistral may get right only 40%\n    of the time. This differential item functioning (DIF) was hidden in single-model\n    analyses where it was confounded with the residual. The 33.6pp drop in item_id\n    is dramatic and changes the entire paper narrative.\n\n    Bootstrap CIs are wide (15.3pp for item_id, 14.9pp for model×item) and the two\n    CIs overlap, meaning we cannot even definitively say which is larger. This is\n    driven by item-level resampling with only 200 items — each bootstrap resample\n    has ~127 unique items (Euler''s formula), and the item×model interaction is sensitive\n    to which items are included. The width exceeds 12pp on all benchmarks, making\n    confident rank-ordering of variance sources impossible on most benchmarks.\n\n    D-study projections are dire: achieving G≥0.90 under binary scoring requires 1400\n    items on MMLU, 2000 on GSM8K (practically unreachable — GSM8K only has 1319 test\n    items), and 980-1200 on ARC/HellaSwag. These budgets are 3-10× larger than MVP\n    estimates because model×item interaction adds a variance source that cannot be\n    reduced by prompt/seed averaging — only item expansion helps. The cross-benchmark\n    range (45-2000 items) is so large that no single recommendation is actionable.\n\n    The G-theory vs IRT comparison reveals a 12.7× discrepancy (tinyBenchmarks: 110\n    items; G-theory: 1400 items). This is because IRT ignores prompt/seed/temperature/\n    ordering facets entirely — it assumes a single fixed evaluation protocol. The\n    G-theory budget accounts for reproducibility across ALL facets, making it much\n    larger. This is actually a strong finding: IRT-based subsampling produces reliable\n    rankings only within a fixed protocol, not across protocols.\n\n    Fixed vs random sensitivity analysis (MD2) shows a 7.2pp maximum change when temperature\n    is treated as fixed vs random. Temperature has only 2-3 design points, making\n    the random assumption dubious. But the fixed treatment changes item_id by 4.1pp,\n    which is large enough to affect conclusions. This is a genuine methodological\n    vulnerability with no clean resolution.\n\n    The Bernoulli correlation analysis (MD1) confirms that 61% of item_id''s magnitude\n    is mechanically driven by item difficulty spread through p(1-p). This is damning:\n    more than half of the \"headline finding\" (item dominance) is a statistical artifact\n    of using binary scoring on items with heterogeneous difficulty. The text_match\n    metric does not suffer from this, but text_match has different (and less dramatic)\n    variance structure.\n\n    Even projecting to 14K items (full MMLU), binary G reaches only 0.891 [0.847,\n    0.934], still below 0.90 with a CI that includes values as low as 0.847. This\n    means even full MMLU may not provide reliable binary-scored evaluation when considering\n    all facets — a provocative but hard-to-communicate finding.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 8: Model-as-Facet Variance Decomposition (MMLU, binary scoring)'\n    description: 'The central table of the paper. Rows: item_id (22.4%), model (6.8%),\n      model×item (31.2%), prompt×item (4.8%), temp×item (5.2%), precision×item (2.1%),\n      seed (0.4%), prompt (1.2%), temperature (2.8%), precision (4.3%), 3-way+ interactions\n      (4.2%), residual (11.9%). Model×item is largest. Bold. Adjacent column: bootstrap\n      95% CI for each. CIs on item_id and model×item overlap.\n\n      '\n  - name: 'Table 9: Model-as-Facet Variance Decomposition Across Benchmarks'\n    description: 'Same format as Table 8 but columns = MMLU, GSM8K, ARC, HellaSwag.\n      Model×item is largest or co-largest on MMLU (31.2%) and GSM8K (28.7%). On ARC,\n      item_id remains largest (34.1%) with model×item at 22.3%. HellaSwag: prompt×item\n      (18.9%) competes with model×item (21.7%). No consistent pattern across benchmarks.\n\n      '\n  - name: 'Table 10: D-Study Projections for G≥0.90 (Binary Scoring)'\n    description: 'Rows: MMLU, GSM8K, ARC, HellaSwag. Columns: required items, prompts,\n      seeds, total conditions, estimated GPU-hours, USD cost. Items range 980-2000.\n      GSM8K marked \"exceeds available test items.\" Total costs: $4.2K-$11.5K per benchmark.\n\n      '\n  - name: 'Table 11: G-theory vs IRT Item Recommendations'\n    description: 'Comparison table. IRT (tinyBenchmarks): 110 items. G-theory: 1400\n      items. Ratio: 12.7×. Explanation: IRT assumes fixed protocol; G-theory targets\n      cross-protocol reliability. Both are \"correct\" for different reliability definitions.\n\n      '\n  - name: 'Table 12: Bootstrap CI Widths by Variance Source and Benchmark'\n    description: 'All variance sources × all benchmarks. CI widths range 3-16pp. Sources\n      with CI >12pp highlighted. Item_id and model×item exceed 12pp on all benchmarks.\n      Precision main effect CIs are tighter (3-5pp). Message: only main effects can\n      be estimated precisely; interactions and item-dependent sources are noisy.\n\n      '\n  - name: 'Table 13: Methodological Sensitivity Analyses Summary'\n    description: 'Rows: REML vs Henderson, Fixed vs Random, Binary vs TextMatch, 3×3\n      vs 2×2, Bootstrap vs Jackknife. Columns: max difference (pp), rank order preserved\n      (Y/N), conclusion. Two of five analyses show >5pp sensitivity. Overall: results\n      are moderately robust but not insensitive to analytic choices.\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 3 (SIGNATURE): Stacked-bar variance decomposition, single-model vs model-as-facet,\n    side by side for MMLU. Dramatic visual: item_id shrinks, model×item emerges as\n    largest block. The ''before and after'' is the paper''s most striking visual.'\n  - 'Figure 4: Stacked-bar variance decomposition across all 4 benchmarks (model-as-facet).\n    Each bar has a unique ''fingerprint.'' No gradient visible.'\n  - 'Figure 5: D-study projection curves — G vs item count for 4 benchmarks. Curves\n    diverge wildly. GSM8K never reaches G=0.90 within available items. MMLU reaches\n    0.90 at ~1400 items.'\n  - 'Figure 6: Bootstrap CI forest plot for top-5 variance sources on MMLU. Wide,\n    overlapping CIs for item_id and model×item. Tight CIs for precision main effect.'\n  - 'Figure 7: Bernoulli p(1-p) vs observed item variance scatter plot. r=0.83 correlation.\n    Regression line shows 61% of item_id is mechanical.'\n  analysis: 'exp-004 reveals that the entire single-model analysis framework was misleading.\n    Model×item interaction (31.2%) — not item difficulty — is the dominant variance\n    source when models are properly crossed. This is the worst-tier''s central finding\n    and it is genuinely novel and surprising. However, the wide bootstrap CIs, the\n    Bernoulli mechanical artifact, and the benchmark-idiosyncratic patterns make the\n    paper very hard to write cleanly. Every claim requires heavy qualification. The\n    D-study budgets are impractically large and benchmark-specific, providing no actionable\n    \"recipe.\" The G-theory vs IRT comparison is the strongest standalone contribution\n    but is a methods comparison, not an empirical finding. The narrative must shift\n    to: \"evaluation is harder than you think, and here''s exactly why.\"\n\n    '\n- name: exp-005 14B Model Extension\n  plan_id: exp-005\n  description: 'Qwen3-14B and DeepSeek-R1-Distill-14B at BF16/FP16. Tests scale effects.\n    Depends on exp-003 (hardware). In worst tier, hardware issues cascade.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    hardware_obtained: false\n    experiment_status: blocked — requires ≥48GB VRAM datacenter GPU not available\n    alt_single_gpu_status: attempted on westd-32570 (96GB) with reduced design\n    alt_total_records: 28800\n    alt_qwen14b_accuracy: 0.738\n    alt_deepseek_r1_distill_accuracy: 0.712\n    alt_item_id_pct_qwen14b: 0.501\n    alt_item_id_pct_deepseek_r1: 0.423\n    alt_cross_scale_item_correlation: 0.64\n    alt_model_x_item_estimated: not estimable with 2-model design\n  result_rationale: 'exp-005 is either blocked entirely (no datacenter GPU) or runs\n    on the single available GPU with a severely reduced design. Even if it runs, 2\n    models is insufficient to estimate model×item interaction as a random effect —\n    you need ≥3 for meaningful variance component estimation. The cross-scale item\n    difficulty correlation (r=0.64 between 8B and 14B models) is lower than within-scale\n    correlation (r=0.71-0.78 among 8B models), suggesting that scale increases model×item\n    interaction further. This is consistent with the worst-tier narrative (larger\n    models have more distinct item-level competence profiles) but the evidence is\n    too thin to make claims. The experiment contributes almost nothing to the paper\n    in the worst tier.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table A1 (Appendix): 14B Model Accuracy Summary'\n    description: 'Minimal 2×2 table (2 models × 2 precisions). Qwen3-14B: 73.8%, DeepSeek-R1-Distill:\n      71.2%. Noted as \"reduced design, insufficient for G-theory analysis.\"\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Scatter plot: 8B vs 14B item-level accuracy. r=0.64. Points dispersed more than\n    within-scale comparisons. Suggestive but underpowered.'\n  analysis: 'exp-005 is the clearest casualty of the worst tier. It either doesn''t\n    run or produces too little data to support claims. Relegated to a brief appendix\n    mention or dropped entirely. The cross-scale correlation finding (r=0.64) is interesting\n    but requires follow-up work with more models and a full crossing design.\n\n    '\n- name: exp-006 Fine-Tuning G-Study\n  plan_id: exp-006\n  description: 'RoBERTa-large on SST-2/MNLI/RTE/COPA. 6 seeds × 3 learning rates ×\n    4 data shuffles × 3 GPUs × 2 precisions = 432 runs/task × 4 tasks = 1,728 total\n    runs. Independent of inference experiments. Tests fine-tuning variance hierarchy.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    total_runs_completed: 1614\n    total_runs_planned: 1728\n    failure_rate: 0.066\n    failed_config: lr=5e-4 × fp16 on RTE/COPA (small datasets, unstable)\n    item_id_pct_sst2: 0.312\n    seed_pct_sst2: 0.089\n    lr_pct_sst2: 0.143\n    data_shuffle_pct_sst2: 0.034\n    gpu_pct_sst2: 0.002\n    precision_pct_sst2: 0.018\n    seed_x_lr_pct_sst2: 0.112\n    residual_pct_sst2: 0.178\n    g_coeff_sst2: 0.487\n    item_id_pct_mnli: 0.401\n    lr_pct_mnli: 0.098\n    seed_pct_mnli: 0.067\n    seed_x_item_pct_mnli: 0.134\n    residual_pct_mnli: 0.156\n    g_coeff_mnli: 0.534\n    item_id_pct_rte: 0.187\n    seed_pct_rte: 0.203\n    lr_pct_rte: 0.178\n    seed_x_lr_pct_rte: 0.156\n    residual_pct_rte: 0.214\n    g_coeff_rte: 0.298\n    item_id_pct_copa: 0.134\n    seed_pct_copa: 0.241\n    lr_pct_copa: 0.198\n    residual_pct_copa: 0.267\n    g_coeff_copa: 0.213\n    inference_vs_finetuning_rank_correlation: 0.31\n    dominant_source_inference: item_id (or model×item)\n    dominant_source_finetuning: item_id on large datasets; seed on small datasets\n  result_rationale: 'Fine-tuning variance hierarchy differs substantially from inference.\n    On large datasets (SST-2, MNLI), item_id is still the largest source (31-40%)\n    but learning rate and seed×learning_rate interactions are substantial (10-14%),\n    which have no analogue in inference. On small datasets (RTE: 277 items; COPA:\n    100 items), seed becomes dominant (20-24%) because the training dynamics are chaotic\n    with few examples — small perturbations in initialization cascade through training.\n\n    GPU and precision contribute negligibly to fine-tuning variance (<2% combined),\n    which contrasts with the 4-7% precision contribution in inference. This is because\n    fine-tuning aggregates over many gradient steps, averaging out per-forward-pass\n    precision effects.\n\n    The cross-paradigm rank correlation (r=0.31) between inference and fine-tuning\n    variance hierarchies means the findings do NOT transfer. A reviewer cannot extrapolate\n    from our inference G-study to fine-tuning practice, and we cannot extrapolate\n    from exp-006 back to inference. This supports the worst-tier narrative (\"nothing\n    generalizes\") but also means exp-006 is somewhat disconnected from the paper''s\n    main story. It becomes an appendix-level extension rather than a core contribution.\n\n    The 6.6% run failure rate (NaN loss on high-lr×fp16 combinations with small datasets)\n    is a methodological nuisance. These failures are informative (they show that some\n    precision×hyperparameter combinations are unstable) but create missing cells in\n    the G-theory design, requiring imputation or reduced analysis.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 14: Fine-Tuning Variance Decomposition Across Tasks'\n    description: 'Rows: seed, learning_rate, data_shuffle, gpu, precision, item_id,\n      seed×lr, seed×item, residual. Columns: SST-2, MNLI, RTE, COPA. Clear split between\n      large-dataset (item-dominated) and small-dataset (seed-dominated) patterns.\n\n      '\n  - name: 'Table 15: Inference vs Fine-Tuning Variance Hierarchy Comparison'\n    description: 'Side-by-side ranking of variance sources. Inference: item_id > model×item\n      > residual > temperature×item > prompt×item. Fine-tuning: item_id > seed×lr\n      > seed > lr > residual. Different hierarchies, different implications.\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Paired stacked-bar: inference (MMLU) vs fine-tuning (SST-2) variance decomposition.\n    Visually distinct patterns — different colors dominate each bar.'\n  - 'G-coefficient comparison: inference tasks vs fine-tuning tasks. Fine-tuning G\n    is generally higher on large datasets but lower on small datasets.'\n  analysis: 'exp-006 produces a valid standalone result (fine-tuning variance hierarchy\n    differs from inference) but its contribution to the main paper is ambiguous. It\n    supports the worst-tier message (\"nothing generalizes across paradigms\") but also\n    weakens coherence — the paper is already struggling to find a unifying narrative,\n    and adding a disconnected fine-tuning analysis makes it more scattered. Recommend\n    relegating to a 1-page appendix or splitting into a separate short paper.\n\n    '\n- name: exp-007 LayerCast Precision Mitigation\n  plan_id: exp-007\n  description: 'LayerCast (FP16 storage + FP32 compute) as additional precision condition\n    on MMLU. Tests whether this mixed-precision strategy reduces variance from the\n    precision facet.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    total_records: 43200\n    layercast_accuracy: 0.6482\n    layercast_vs_fp32_gap_pp: 4.88\n    layercast_vs_bf16_gap_pp: -3.62\n    precision_variance_with_layercast_pct: 5.1\n    layercast_item_level_correlation_with_bf16: 0.87\n    layercast_item_level_correlation_with_fp32: 0.72\n    variance_reduction_from_layercast: none — adds variance rather than reducing it\n  result_rationale: 'LayerCast does not cleanly reduce variance because it creates\n    a hybrid precision regime that behaves differently from both pure FP32 and pure\n    FP16/BF16. The storage-in-FP16, compute-in-FP32 approach means attention computations\n    use FP32 but weight quantization noise from FP16 storage propagates through. The\n    result is an accuracy level between FP32 and BF16 (64.8%), but with item-level\n    patterns that don''t correlate perfectly with either pure precision. Adding LayerCast\n    as a 4th precision level actually INCREASES precision facet variance (from 4.3%\n    to 5.1%) because it adds another distinct level rather than collapsing precision\n    differences. This is a negative result for the mitigation narrative but a positive\n    result for the worst-tier message: precision effects are complex and non-monotonic,\n    and simple mitigation strategies don''t work as advertised.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table A2 (Appendix): LayerCast Variance Impact'\n    description: '4-row precision comparison (FP32, FP16, BF16, LayerCast) with accuracy,\n      item-level correlation with BF16, and precision facet variance contribution.\n      LayerCast is intermediate but not a clean fix.\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Item-level accuracy scatter: LayerCast vs BF16 (r=0.87) and LayerCast vs FP32\n    (r=0.72). Two panels showing LayerCast is closer to BF16 but not a drop-in replacement.'\n  analysis: 'exp-007 is a minor negative result. LayerCast doesn''t serve as a precision\n    mitigation strategy in this context. Useful as a 1-paragraph appendix note showing\n    that mixed-precision approaches don''t simplify the variance picture. Low priority\n    for inclusion in the worst-tier paper.\n\n    '\n- name: exp-008 MATH Benchmark Extension\n  plan_id: exp-008\n  description: 'MATH benchmark (200 items) with chain-of-thought reasoning. Tests\n    whether reasoning-heavy tasks with long generation have qualitatively different\n    variance structure.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    total_records: 115200\n    models_completed: 2\n    overall_accuracy_llama: 0.341\n    overall_accuracy_qwen: 0.428\n    accuracy_range_across_conditions: 0.187\n    item_id_pct_math: 0.118\n    seed_pct_math: 0.134\n    temperature_pct_math: 0.156\n    prompt_pct_math: 0.087\n    seed_x_item_pct_math: 0.168\n    temperature_x_item_pct_math: 0.143\n    precision_x_temperature_pct_math_binary: 4.7\n    residual_pct_math: 0.194\n    g_coeff_binary_math_200item: 0.152\n    d_study_items_for_g90_math_binary: not achievable within MATH test set size\n    avg_generation_length_tokens: 487\n    generation_length_cv: 0.68\n    accuracy_conditional_on_cot_length: non-monotonic — medium length CoT has highest\n      accuracy\n  result_rationale: 'MATH represents the extreme end of the worst-tier pattern. Item_id\n    is only 11.8% — the lowest of any benchmark — because chain-of-thought generation\n    introduces massive stochastic branching. Each reasoning step is a decision point\n    where temperature and seed can send the model down different solution paths. The\n    seed×item (16.8%) and temperature×item (14.3%) interactions are the dominant sources,\n    meaning the same item can be solved or not depending on the random seed and temperature\n    — an extreme form of the model×item interaction pattern.\n\n    The 18.7pp accuracy range across conditions for the same model on the same items\n    is striking: the \"same\" evaluation can produce 34.1% or 52.8% accuracy for Llama\n    on MATH depending on prompt/seed/temperature. This makes any single-run MATH evaluation\n    essentially meaningless for fine-grained model comparison.\n\n    Separability fails catastrophically: precision×temperature interaction is 4.7%\n    under binary scoring, the highest of any benchmark. This is because FP16/BF16\n    rounding in the softmax creates different token selections at each reasoning step,\n    and at higher temperatures these divergences compound over 400+ tokens of chain-of-thought.\n\n    G=0.152 for 200 items under binary scoring is the lowest across all benchmarks.\n    D-study projections show G≥0.90 is not achievable within MATH''s test set (5000\n    items) because the dominant variance sources (seed×item, temperature×item) cannot\n    be reduced by adding items — they require averaging over many seeds and temperatures\n    per item, which is computationally expensive.\n\n    Mistral''s exclusion (accuracy <10%, effectively random guessing on MATH) further\n    reduces model coverage to 2, making model-as-facet analysis impossible on this\n    benchmark.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table A3 (Appendix): MATH Variance Decomposition'\n    description: 'Seed×item (16.8%) and temperature×item (14.3%) dominate. Item_id\n      is only 11.8%. Precision×temperature is 4.7%. G=0.152. The most extreme interaction-dominated\n      pattern in the study.\n\n      '\n  - name: 'Table A4: Accuracy Range by Model Across Conditions (MATH)'\n    description: '2 models × min/max/range across all conditions. Llama: 22.5-52.8%\n      (range 30.3pp). Qwen: 31.2-58.4% (range 27.2pp). Demonstrates that single-run\n      MATH evaluation is unreliable.\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Heatmap: seed × temperature accuracy grid for Llama on MATH. High variance visible\n    — hot spots and cold spots across the grid with no smooth pattern.'\n  - 'Chain-of-thought length distribution by accuracy outcome (correct vs incorrect).\n    Bimodal: very short (gave up) and very long (went in circles) both predict failure.'\n  analysis: 'MATH is the poster child for the worst-tier narrative: everything interacts,\n    nothing is stable, and single-run evaluation is meaningless. But with only 2 models\n    and a reduced design, the evidence is thin. The extreme interaction dominance\n    is compelling but also predictable (CoT reasoning is inherently stochastic). The\n    finding reinforces the \"no gradient\" message — MATH is not simply \"one step further\"\n    on a knowledge→reasoning continuum; it has a qualitatively different variance\n    structure that shares nothing with MMLU or ARC. Best used as a dramatic closing\n    example in the paper rather than a core analysis.'\n","numLines":777,"startLine":1,"totalLines":777}}}
